diagnostics/list-sort-and-associate.em:1:21: `sort` needs a List of ordered values, but this is [Bool]
  print([true, false].sort())
                      ^^^^
Ints, Floats, Strings, and types that adopt `Ordered` have an order. Use another operation for these elements.
diagnostics/list-sort-and-associate.em:3:21: `sort` cannot order [Int?], whose elements may be absent
  print(maybe_numbers.sort())
                      ^^^^
Use `filter_map` to remove absent values first, or give each element a fallback with `.or(...)`.
diagnostics/list-sort-and-associate.em:4:11: `sort` takes 0 arguments, but this call passes 1
  print([1].sort(1))
            ^^^^
Match the number of arguments to what the method needs.
diagnostics/list-sort-and-associate.em:6:1: `frozen` is a `const`, so its contents cannot change
  frozen.sort!()
  ^^^^^^
Declare `frozen` with `var` if it needs to change.
diagnostics/list-sort-and-associate.em:7:22: this block returns Bool, but `sort_by` needs an ordered key
  print([1, 2].sort_by { number => number.even?() })
                       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
Return an Int, Float, String, or a type that adopts `Ordered`.
diagnostics/list-sort-and-associate.em:8:14: [Int] cannot be a dictionary key
  print([1, 2].unique_by { number => [number] })
               ^^^^^^^^^
A key must be a number, a `Bool`, a `String`, an enum value, or a tuple or struct made only from valid key types. Lists, other mutable collections, and class objects cannot be keys.
diagnostics/list-sort-and-associate.em:9:24: this block returns Int, but `associate` needs a `(key, value)` tuple
  print([1, 2].associate { number => number })
                         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
Return a two-element tuple, as in `items.associate { item => (item.name, item.count) }`.
diagnostics/list-sort-and-associate.em:10:14: `to_dictionary` needs a List of two-element tuples, but this is [Int]
  print([1, 2].to_dictionary())
               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
Use `associate` or `associate_by` to build the key and value from each element instead.
